2186049390 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "f0e26f7e-2461-5845-b570-4028c1709f5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428477 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428478 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1031 "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1033 1945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049676 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428468 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428469 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428470 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1031 "Linearization of Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1033 1937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049809 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "156d2779-eafb-5c80-bd7a-1ed2ab0b5ca6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1031 "Local Extrema of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1033 2707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "27991def-afa0-5245-84f6-cb161dd1a2bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051048 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051048 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051048 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051048 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051048 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428474 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051048 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428475 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051048 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428476 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051048 #edb/ref 1031 "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051048 #edb/ref 1033 1944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051048 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124209 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12460" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124209 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b5/b550d203e3be327eb2af27a1035b6693953dd2802bb203af06d5773e48d609c3.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124209 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "137818ca-6c48-5ae1-8788-2015f7890ad5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124209 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = 2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y) = (0,-2),$ so the global maximum point is $(0,-2,2).$\n\n![](images/f8/f80d19126252f6ab7097cf528aec9726c2708f48c815c026f632fccd77cf74d4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124210 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124210 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6c/6c6906429cf946f196b959cce7c20589b6212ae45df9607e15c6385be9aef858.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124210 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "976e4ec4-93f9-57a4-9f2b-2116e09fed22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124210 #edb/ref 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A, B,$ and $D.$ Hence, $A, B,$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D(x_D,y_D,z_D),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f(x_D,y_D) = f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/cd/cdb83ac891799788c8289f7e44902bf6be008cb13a7bf27f330b48113df021d1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124211 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124211 #edb/ref 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is given by\n![](images/2a/2ae5b42ae953d1b9d3fd642e9938924a9b2bc439dee1ea52c868f186798b1520.png)\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are $\\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3,$ what type of point is $\\mathbf{a}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124211 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94c54743-ba74-51c5-9c59-777125bb76f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124211 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if $\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ In such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ about $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})\n+\n\\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T\n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$ term, we can conclude that $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ Hence, $\\mathbf a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum of $f.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124379 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124379 #edb/ref 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function ![](images/e2/e21f1a654e7ec74a637369e2330d0e0f3ccd0a3469bc15405b9ae581aaa4efba.png) about ![](images/e2/e2a3ebddc9b6755b15eca1e5497dfc859ea5c2d6bfb5ee0a98df1c6476f87a4f.png) is given by\n![](images/a2/a21655b6405345e52c0f18c50e098409ca06e2b938ee77dc1b839c54f5f60456.png)\n\nwhere \n\n![](images/a9/a963b613e053f027bc0434bacaba0056c70493b72181eff33f97cd16d39afc37.png) \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](images/83/8317d25a4b5a486cf86db32d0868ca8135c4701abf2e2f579b59ce7f4c3ec05b.png) and ![](images/5d/5db28cc61ee0aadd0ccc482cacf12c3a851ff803af7927d3bb918bc9c7c72ffc.png)\n- ![](images/8f/8f3cbdf2ea946537b9792aba2720fb22db787b8b72be93a3c0a5308ff111ee13.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- ![](images/42/42f776502d5c552cefc008f7ea701f0f45578326e649b3f4dcd4bc548738d0ef.png) is a saddle point of ![](images/fb/fb7b36c5e6bd7e8c334d8874eb7a781085930a078aadd1cabc79475006fa894c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124379 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6938151-ab77-5288-ba08-156dc942ddec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124379 #edb/ref 1074 "The point ![](images/7a/7ac5ab8e5444901eb7e978a0916e953bbe6da7ff8fb8a481537ad1f07d5bf48d.png) is a critical point of the function ![](images/87/87f22c24143e0fdad241f6b070b0e0ba1d89b2d5c32669989a7c75df700f5e75.png) if ![](images/2f/2fa6429606f96f4c4f43b91de76acab79152fd9989108836084cea77e0d9db09.png) In such cases, we can classify ![](images/12/1287cfe4aa57a8ec4c01f84934cb29b3d524f0524a48e39d7f6a30aed97e8ee2.png) as follows:\n\n- if ![](images/f2/f267e59d3059eac794b9f97c216e0dafd1b01c00ea5a0466d01cdab5fdeef865.png) is positive definite, then ![](images/81/81a814457abc7dc83baedc3d0ccd3be23e5734c3634a0bc8c8ce7fca53cc9406.png) is a local minimum\n\n- if ![](images/91/91ecc7d66921c91ed5baf1b1d2453f9cc4678feed21f325291b38f103f0266b6.png) is negative definite, then ![](images/74/74bbb71a684ae2b42d8a3779704a0c8b47ef43e5cebd265861418f77cd2b6267.png) is a local maximum\n\n- if ![](images/f2/f267e59d3059eac794b9f97c216e0dafd1b01c00ea5a0466d01cdab5fdeef865.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then ![](images/81/81a814457abc7dc83baedc3d0ccd3be23e5734c3634a0bc8c8ce7fca53cc9406.png) is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have ![](images/1a/1ada0c06e92bc2e76f33fade0b4f28728f641cc98923f855735c118d2ea0ba25.png) Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ![](images/29/29aa6115eeac65ed8ca2194e8fdce4de48591b144de9ee298e7c151f78bda4ac.png) for ![](images/ff/ffb48b96eb46bc28ce8546552edbc23496ae84720dc2cc4413cc7425b056bba0.png)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](images/36/363026acf3cf01ffee6edd4166c849b1cce0cfd1e14af8dc3a15fa2a2f41163d.png) matrix ![](images/50/50946bb3afa72276eb132efe89bbe450df8836747fa6d11d04dbb142da7f6fad.png) Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](images/40/407d3bd58712c649860e47e39322f4471da8446ed312741900b2697fafe9d4a5.png) (as well as the matrix ![](images/84/849d81b747384fa1c4d5194327dd049207fdb7bf2db258e117c7214f870555b3.png)) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](images/a9/a93d47764597bd39da3f5bb625a6d1977c61f6a731e8b041850fef5dc9c7bc52.png) for all ![](images/81/8161c7c2b38d84c7033c688b3b41d0decc2861cd218f08dc3f87625eed2cc376.png)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](images/12/127d0aace5f471d897e0b6171e68da877234f9b709bf379245032522de9f6aa5.png) we have ![](images/b1/b18d3603f870bb527a4ee4618ed482165e62659a001cf8f2bbef4503c249b313.png) when ![](images/54/541f4f6d68d831274235815dceef666c2a59172ab8825d63aa189957530d9ce9.png) is odd, and ![](images/32/32a94e6ea1436d78a428c8ff22e50a7df311609ab74824c672abd2334c83988a.png) when ![](images/ae/aebdc0a3ecd72c42a452c68c6019eff7ad41de49b8a09517f7fd2ff8df58e32f.png) is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ![](images/fc/fcb57eaada70434abbf4a6056c9a8a793f6a0348196813e6a512de4772290f8e.png) but\n![](images/ae/ae6614c135f2e9d1d011de5f58d17fb4e43c2c6b87a7831a57b1287cf1a6e13d.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n![](images/18/1851f51854993b0360e75b0fc717263a7a84938b86ae515d74be32a0ac87362d.png)\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix ![](images/79/7906e975aaeffa8eae6d319b4be2d6a585c41b95ec59d721eedb7355fda29066.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix ![](images/33/3397629141c3034fbd4b5cd5bd8c554676175a4b1e3f5eccd26cc5d66ee1bec8.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point ![](images/27/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) is a saddle point of ![](images/4f/4f118f558380017fb67aa8f0b030dfa06a10216b7381582c619907c32988f211.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124570 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124646 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124646 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124646 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124646 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1